            <h1>Kerberos Delegation &amp; Enumeration</h1>
<h4>Unconstrained</h4>
<p>Having established a beacon/session on SRV01 as the user <code>svc_sql</=
code>, we would further enumerate the domain utilizing <code>sharpsh</code>=
. The target (SRV01) may sometimes only reach some ports or specific ports =
on our Sliver C2 server due to network segmentation or firewall restriction=
s. Fortunately, having a looser network and firewall restrictions in the sa=
me subnet is common. We could utilize <a href=3D"https://sliver.sh/docs?nam=
e=3DPort+Forwarding" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener nofollow">reverse po=
rt forwarding</a> functionality to bypass various network restrictions.</p>
<p>Assume that we set up a file server on port 8080 on our attack machine, =
and the target cannot reach this port. However, it can reach port 8080 on W=
EB01; we can create a reverse port forwarding on WEB01 to relay the traffic=
 between SRV01 and our attack machine. To circumvent those types of restric=
tions, we will utilize the reverse port forwarding (<code>rportfwd</code>) =
functionality in Sliver. Execute the following command in the session of WE=
B01:</p>
<div class=3D"window_container"><div class=3D"window_content">
                      <div class=3D"window_top">
                          <span class=3D"window_dot bg-danger"></span>
                          <span class=3D"window_dot bg-warning"></span>
                          <span class=3D"window_dot bg-success"></span>
                          <span class=3D"window_title">Kerberos Delegation =
&amp; Enumeration</span>
                      </div>
                  <pre class=3D" language-shell-session" style=3D"line-heig=
ht: 0px;"><code class=3D" language-shell-session"><span class=3D"token outp=
ut">sliver (http-beacon) &gt; rportfwd add -b 8080 -r 127.0.0.1:8080

[*] Reverse port forwarding 127.0.0.1:8080 &lt;- :8080
</span></code></pre></div></div>
<p>Having established reverse port forwarding on WEB01, we will now utilize=
 the PsExec session on SRV01. Levering the <code>sharpsh</code> route expla=
ined in the <code>Domain Reconnaissance</code> section, we will proceed to =
query information about the delegation (Unconstrained). We will briefly tou=
ch on the topic, however, if you need to become more familiar with it, chec=
k out the <a href=3D"https://academy.hackthebox.com/module/details/25" targ=
et=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener nofollow">Kerberos Attacks</a> module.</p>
<p>Kerberos delegation enables a user or service to act on behalf of anothe=
r user to another service. A typical scenario is that a user authenticates =
to the IIS server, and the IIS server acts on behalf of the user to authent=
icate to an MSSQL server.</p>
<p>Unconstrained delegation can be assigned to a computer or user, mostly a=
 computer. The configuration can be done on the domain controller. From a s=
ystem administrator's perspective, we can select the Trust this computer fo=
r delegation to any service (Kerberos only) option on the Delegation tab to=
 configure unconstrained delegation for a domain computer.</p>
<p><img src=3D"https://academy.hackthebox.com/storage/modules/241/SRV01_del=
eg.png" alt=3D"SRV01 Properties window showing delegation settings, with op=
tions for trusting the computer for delegation to any service using Kerbero=
s."></p>
<p>Considering the <a href=3D"https://academy.hackthebox.com/module/143/sec=
tion/1573" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener nofollow">double hop</a> probl=
em, how did unconstrained delegation resolved it? If a computer is configur=
ed with unconstrained delegation, as the user accesses the IIS (front-end s=
ervice) server, the KDC also includes the user=E2=80=99s TGT to TGS ticket.=
 Then, the IIS server extracts the user=E2=80=99s TGT and caches it in memo=
ry. After that, the IIS server uses the user=E2=80=99s TGT to act on behalf=
 of the user to access the MSSQL (back-end service) server. But the issue i=
s that since the user=E2=80=99s TGT is cached in the IIS server=E2=80=99s m=
emory, the IIS server can use the user=E2=80=99s TGT to act on behalf of th=
e user to any other service, which means the IIS server impersonates the us=
er. If the IIS server is compromised, the attacker can extract all TGT from=
 memory and impersonate these users. What=E2=80=99s worse is that if a high=
-privileged user=E2=80=99s TGT is cached, such as a domain administrator=E2=
=80=99s, the attacker can take over the whole domain and forest.</p>
<p>Let's explain the steps in detail.</p>
<div class=3D"table-responsive"><table class=3D"table table-striped text-le=
ft">
<thead>
<tr>
<th><strong>Step</strong></th>
<th><strong>Description</strong></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Step 1: User to DC</td>
<td>The user requests a TGT.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Step 2: DC to user</td>
<td>The user gets the TGT.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Step 3: User to DC</td>
<td>The user requests a TGS ticket.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Step 4: DC to user</td>
<td>The user gets the TGS ticket.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Step 5: User to the IIS server</td>
<td>The user sends both the TGT and TGS ticket.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Step 6: IIS server to DC</td>
<td>The IIS server uses the user=E2=80=99s TGT to request a TGS ticket to D=
C to access the MSSQL server.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Step 7: IIS server to MSSQL server</td>
<td>The IIS server acts on behalf of the user to access the MSSQL server.</=
td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Let's proceed with the enumeration of the unconstrained delegation. Refe=
rring to the approach in the Domain Reconnaissance section, we will base64 =
encode the query (command).</p>
<div class=3D"window_container"><div class=3D"window_content">
                      <div class=3D"window_top">
                          <span class=3D"window_dot bg-danger"></span>
                          <span class=3D"window_dot bg-warning"></span>
                          <span class=3D"window_dot bg-success"></span>
